#### Pitfall 6: No Monitoring of mTLS Handshake Failures

**Problem:** mTLS failures go unnoticed until services can't communicate, no visibility into root cause.

**Solution:** Log and monitor all TLS handshake failures.

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD: Comprehensive TLS failure logging
options.Events = new CertificateAuthenticationEvents
{
    OnAuthenticationFailed = context =>
    {
        _logger.LogError(
            "Client certificate authentication failed: {Error}, " +
            "Client IP: {ClientIP}, Certificate: {Subject}",
            context.Exception?.Message,
            context.HttpContext.Connection.RemoteIpAddress,
            context.ClientCertificate?.Subject ?? "None");

        // Emit metric for monitoring
        _metrics.IncrementCounter("mtls_auth_failures", new Dictionary<string, string>
        {
            { "error_type", context.Exception?.GetType().Name ?? "Unknown" },
            { "has_certificate", (context.ClientCertificate != null).ToString() }
        });

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }
};
```

